---
description: |
  Detects credential stuffing and password spraying in OCSF Authentication.

  The operator aggregates logons per source network in tumbling event-time
  windows and reports a source when its failed logons reach `min_failures`,
  span at least `min_users` distinct accounts, and make up at least
  `min_failure_share` of its logons. Many accounts with few attempts each
  separate credential stuffing and password spraying from a forgotten
  password. Successful logons from a reported source raise the severity from
  medium to high and appear as user evidence, because they are accounts whose
  credentials worked for the attacker.

  The operator considers logons (`activity_id` `1`), reads `status_id` to tell
  success (`1`) from failure (`2`), and identifies accounts by `user.name`,
  `user.email_addr`, or `user.uid`, in that order. Events without a source IP
  address, such as local logons, don't count. Sources aggregate to IPv4
  addresses and IPv6 `/64` networks.

  Known false negatives:

  - Distributed attacks that spread across many unrelated networks or stay
    below `min_failures` per window from each one.
  - Brute force against a single account, which needs a per-account
    detection instead.
  - Logons that the identity provider rejects before it records an
    Authentication event, for example when a bot challenge or rate limit
    blocks the request.
  - Correct passwords that end in an MFA challenge, when the source reports
    the challenge as a failure, such as Microsoft Entra ID error codes `50074`
    and `50076`. They count as failures and don't raise the severity.
  - Bursts that straddle a window boundary and stay below the thresholds on
    both sides. A burst of at least twice `min_failures` failures within
    `window_size` always reaches the threshold in one window.

  Known false positives:

  - Shared egress such as NAT, CGNAT, a VPN concentrator, or a proxy, when
    many of its users fail at once, for example after a forced password reset
    when devices keep retrying cached credentials.
  - Security tests and misconfigured integrations that iterate over accounts.
  - Several records for the same logon, which inflate the counts and can turn
    a policy or SSO event into an apparent success. Okta, for example, logs
    `user.session.start`, `policy.evaluate_sign_on`, and
    `user.authentication.verify` for one sign-in. Feed one record per attempt,
    such as only `user.session.start` for Okta.

  The operator can't tell credential stuffing from password spraying, because
  logs don't reveal the tried passwords, so findings carry both techniques.
args:
  named:
    - name: window_size
      type: duration
      default: 10min
      description: Event-time window over which to count logons.
    - name: tolerance
      type: duration
      default: 1min
      description: Maximum expected lateness for out-of-order events.
    - name: idle_timeout
      type: duration
      default: 5min
      description: Inactivity after which the current window closes.
    - name: min_failures
      type: int
      default: 20
      description: Minimum number of failed logons from one source network.
    - name: min_users
      type: int
      default: 10
      description: Minimum number of distinct accounts with failed logons.
    - name: min_failure_share
      type: float
      default: 0.8
      description: Minimum share of failed logons among all logons.
---

let $analytic = "tenzir::detect::authentication::credential_stuffing"

where class_uid == 3002 and
      activity_id == 1 and
      status_id in [1, 2] and
      src_endpoint.ip != null
// The summarize key must be a field, so compute the source network once here.
// The account identifier feeds two aggregations, so compute it once as well.
source = subnet(src_endpoint.ip, 64 if src_endpoint.ip.is_v6() else 32)
account = user.name? else user.email_addr? else user.uid?
window size=$window_size, on=time, tolerance=$tolerance,
       idle_timeout=$idle_timeout {
  summarize source,
            logons=count(),
            failures=count_if(status_id, x => x == 2),
            successes=count_if(status_id, x => x == 1),
            failed_users=count_distinct(account if status_id == 2),
            compromised=distinct(user if status_id == 1 and account != null),
            ips=distinct(src_endpoint.ip),
            first=min(time),
            last=max(time)
  start = $window.start
}
where failures >= $min_failures and
      failed_users >= $min_users and
      failures >= logons * $min_failure_share
origin = f"{ips[0] if ips.length() == 1 else source}"
this = {
  time: now(),
  metadata: {
    product: {name: "Tenzir", vendor_name: "Tenzir"},
    uid: uuid(),
    version: "1.9.0",
  },
  category_uid: 2,
  class_uid: 2004,
  activity_id: 1,
  type_uid: 200401,
  severity_id: 4 if successes > 0 else 3,
  status_id: 1,
  is_alert: true,
  start_time: first,
  end_time: last,
  finding_info: {
    uid: f"{$analytic}:{source}:{start}",
    title: f"Credential stuffing from {origin}",
    desc: f"{failures} of {logons} logons from {origin} failed across {failed_users} accounts and {successes} succeeded; source addresses: {ips.length()}",
    analytic: {
      name: "Credential stuffing",
      uid: $analytic,
      type_id: 1,
      version: "1.0",
    },
    attacks: [
      {
        tactic: {uid: "TA0006", name: "Credential Access"},
        technique: {uid: "T1110", name: "Brute Force"},
        sub_technique: {uid: "T1110.004", name: "Credential Stuffing"},
      },
      {
        tactic: {uid: "TA0006", name: "Credential Access"},
        technique: {uid: "T1110", name: "Brute Force"},
        sub_technique: {uid: "T1110.003", name: "Password Spraying"},
      },
    ],
  },
  evidences: [
    ...ips.sort().slice(end=10).map(ip => {src_endpoint: {ip: ip}}),
    ...compromised.slice(end=10).map(user => {user: user}),
  ],
}
